{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon EKS and Amazon EKS Distro now supports Kubernetes version 1.33 - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) and Amazon EKS Distro now support Kubernetes version 1.33. This version includes new features such as stable support for sidecar containers, topology-aware routing and traffic distribution, consideration of taints and tolerations for pod topology spread constraints, user namespaces within Linux pods, dynamic resource allocation for network interfaces, and in-place resource resizing for vertical scaling of pods","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/05/amazon-eks-distro-kubernetes-version-1-33/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon EKS and Amazon EKS Distro now supports Kubernetes version 1.34 - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) and Amazon EKS Distro now support Kubernetes version 1.34. Users can create new EKS clusters using version 1.34 and upgrade existing clusters through the EKS console, eksctl command line interface, or infrastructure-as-code tools. Kubernetes version 1.34 introduces several new features and bug fixes, including projected service account tokens for improved security, Pod-level resource requests and limits for simplified resource management, and Dynamic Resource Allocation (DRA) prioritized alternatives for improved resource scheduling. EKS now supports Kubernetes version 1.34 in all regions where EKS is available, and EKS Distro builds of Kubernetes version 1.34 are available through ECR Public Gallery and GitHub. To learn more about the changes in Kubernetes version 1.34 and instructions to update clusters, visit EKS documentation. EKS cluster insights can be used to check for potential issues impacting cluster upgrades","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/10/amazon-eks-distro-kubernetes-version-1-34/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"aws-containers","skill_description":"Builds, deploys, debugs, and optimizes containerized workloads on Amazon EKS, ECS, Fargate, ECR, and Elastic Beanstalk. Covers EKS operations and add-ons; ECS task definitions, services, Exec, Express Mode, Managed Instances, and Action Logs; ECR repositories and lifecycle policies; and Elastic Beanstalk platforms plus Beanstalk Cluster Mode for running containerized applications on service-managed EKS compute. Use for container deployment, service selection, configuration, scaling, health, troubleshooting, image workflows, or deciding between Beanstalk-managed Cluster Mode and direct EKS. Should be used instead of relying on internal knowledge for these services.\n\nServices: ecs, ecr, eks, apprunner, beanstalk\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: containers, compute, kubernetes","skill_name":"aws-containers"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"amazon-keyspaces","skill_description":"Provides authoritative compatibility checks, pricing estimates, connection troubleshooting, pre-warming guidance, and infrastructure mutations for Amazon Keyspaces (for Apache Cassandra). Covers LWT/batch operations, secondary indexes, materialized views, capacity modes, TTL, PITR, CDC, auto-scaling, multi-region keyspaces, UDTs, nodetool diagnostics parsing, SQL-to-Cassandra migration, and Cassandra-to-Keyspaces migration scenarios. Agents frequently produce incomplete or incorrect answers about Keyspaces feature support without this skill loaded.\n\nServices: keyspaces, cassandra\nTasks: cost-estimation, migration-assessment, compatibility-check, data-modeling, create, modify\nPersona: developer, devops, architect, dba\nWorkload: nosql, wide-column-store, cassandra-migration","skill_name":"amazon-keyspaces"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"managing-amazon-msk","skill_description":"Operates Amazon MSK Provisioned clusters (Standard and Express brokers). Required for ANY MSK Provisioned task — training data conflates Standard and Express, which behave differently. Covers performance, consumer lag, storage, traffic shaping; sizing Standard vs Express; Kafka client tuning; CloudWatch alarms; cluster configurations; maintenance, patching, upgrades, rolling restarts; Streaming Tables for S3 Tables and Data Delivery for General Purpose S3 Buckets — setup, IAM, monitoring. Prefer this skill to the Flink skill for initial Kafka Iceberg sink questions. Triggers: MSK Provisioned (Express/Standard), Kafka, `kafka.*` or `express.*` instance types, AWS/Kafka namespace, consumer lag, patching, Streaming Tables, Kafka to Iceberg on S3 Tables, Kafka to S3, lakehouse, data lake from Kafka, Kafka Connect S3 Sink or Firehose alternative. DO NOT USE for MSK Connect or Replicator — search documentation instead. Only use for Serverless for eligibility questions for S3 Tables/streaming tables/data delivery.\n\n\nServices: msk, kafka, cloudwatch\nTasks: troubleshoot, debug, optimize, deploy, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: streaming, data-analytics","skill_name":"managing-amazon-msk"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify whether AL2 EKS AMIs are supported on EKS 1.33"}

